## 3. Riemannian Geometry for Neural Feature Extraction

All operations in this section are performed identically on each user’s edge device.

### 3.1 Covariance Matrix Estimation

After band‑pass filtering (0.5–50 Hz, 5th‑order Butterworth) and Independent Component Analysis (ICA) for ocular artifact removal, the cleaned signal \(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_i(t)\) is segmented into overlapping windows of length \(L = 125\) samples (500 ms). The sample covariance matrix is

\[
\mathbf{C}_i(t) = \frac{1}{L-1} \tilde{\mathbf{X}}_t \tilde{\mathbf{X}}_t^\top + 10^{-4} \mathbf{I} \;\in\; \mathcal{P}(N_c),
\]

where \(\mathcal{P}(N_c)\) is the manifold of \(N_c \times N_c\) symmetric positive definite matrices. The regularisation term ensures strict positive definiteness and numerical stability.

### 3.2 Affine‑Invariant Riemannian Metric

The manifold \(\mathcal{P}(N_c)\) is equipped with the affine‑invariant Riemannian metric, which endows it with a natural distance that is invariant to affine transformations of the underlying signals:

\[
\delta_R(\mathbf{C}_1, \mathbf{C}_2) = \big\| \log\big( \mathbf{C}_1^{-1/2} \mathbf{C}_2 \mathbf{C}_1^{-1/2} \big) \big\|_F,
\]

where \(\|\cdot\|_F\) is the Frobenius norm and \(\log\) is the matrix logarithm. The tangent space at a point \(\mathbf{C} \in \mathcal{P}(N_c)\) is the set of symmetric matrices, with inner product

\[
\langle \boldsymbol{\xi}, \boldsymbol{\eta} \rangle_{\mathbf{C}} = \operatorname{tr}\big( \mathbf{C}^{-1} \boldsymbol{\xi} \mathbf{C}^{-1} \boldsymbol{\eta} \big).
\]

### 3.3 Tangent Space Projection and Distortion Bound

For each user, we compute a personal reference point \(\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref},i}\) as the Riemannian mean (geometric mean) of that user’s calibration covariance matrices. The logarithmic map at \(\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref},i}\) projects any covariance matrix onto the tangent space:

\[
\mathbf{S}_i(t) = \log_{\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref},i}}\!\big( \mathbf{C}_i(t) \big) = \mathbf{C}_{\text{ref},i}^{1/2} \, \log\!\big( \mathbf{C}_{\text{ref},i}^{-1/2} \mathbf{C}_i(t) \mathbf{C}_{\text{ref},i}^{-1/2} \big) \, \mathbf{C}_{\text{ref},i}^{1/2}.
\]

We then vectorize the upper triangular part of \(\mathbf{S}_i(t)\), scaling the off‑diagonal elements by \(\sqrt{2}\) to preserve the Frobenius norm, producing a feature vector \(\mathbf{X}_i(t) \in \mathbb{R}^d\) with \(d = N_c(N_c+1)/2\). For \(N_c=64\), \(d = 2080\).

The logarithmic map is a diffeomorphism only within the injectivity radius of the manifold. To quantify the distortion introduced by the tangent projection, we use the sectional curvature bound. The maximum sectional curvature of \(\mathcal{P}(N_c)\) under the affine‑invariant metric is \(1/2\). For any \(\mathbf{C}\) with \(\delta_R(\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}, \mathbf{C}) \le r\), the distance in the tangent space approximates the true Riemannian distance with error:

\[
\big| \|\mathbf{X}\|_2 - \delta_R(\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}, \mathbf{C}) \big| \le \frac{1}{6} \kappa_{\max} r^3 + o(r^3) = \frac{r^3}{12} + o(r^3).
\]

In BCI sessions, typical distances are \(r \approx 2\), giving a cubic error term of about \(0.33\), negligible relative to the Euclidean norm of \(\mathbf{X}\) (typically \(10\)–\(30\)). This bound, empirically validated by Barachant & Bonnet (2011), ensures that the tangent space representation preserves the Riemannian distance with high fidelity for all practical EEG covariance matrices.

### 3.4 Online Normalization

Each dimension of \(\mathbf{X}_i(t)\) is standardized online using exponential moving averages of the mean and variance with a forgetting factor of 0.95. This step stabilizes the subsequent neural network training and compensates for slow electrode impedance drifts.

