## Appendix A: Riemannian Geometry of SPD Matrices and Tangent Space Distortion

### A.1 The Manifold of Symmetric Positive Definite Matrices

Let \(\mathcal{P}(n)\) denote the set of \(n \times n\) real symmetric positive definite matrices. \(\mathcal{P}(n)\) is an open convex cone in the vector space of symmetric matrices, and when equipped with the affine‑invariant Riemannian metric, it becomes a Hadamard manifold (simply connected, complete, with non‑positive sectional curvature).

**Tangent space:** For any \(\mathbf{C} \in \mathcal{P}(n)\), the tangent space \(T_{\mathbf{C}}\mathcal{P}(n)\) is isomorphic to the space of symmetric matrices \(\mathcal{S}(n)\). The affine‑invariant metric is defined by

\[
\langle \boldsymbol{\xi}, \boldsymbol{\eta} \rangle_{\mathbf{C}} = \operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{C}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\xi} \mathbf{C}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\eta}), \quad \boldsymbol{\xi},\boldsymbol{\eta} \in T_{\mathbf{C}}\mathcal{P}(n).
\]

**Geodesic distance:** The Riemannian distance between \(\mathbf{C}_1,\mathbf{C}_2 \in \mathcal{P}(n)\) is

\[
\delta_R(\mathbf{C}_1,\mathbf{C}_2) = \| \log(\mathbf{C}_1^{-1/2} \mathbf{C}_2 \mathbf{C}_1^{-1/2}) \|_F,
\]

where \(\|\cdot\|_F\) is the Frobenius norm and \(\log\) denotes the principal matrix logarithm.

**Exponential and logarithmic maps:** At a base point \(\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}\),

\[
\text{Exp}_{\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}}(\mathbf{S}) = \mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}^{1/2} \exp(\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}^{-1/2} \mathbf{S} \mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}^{-1/2}) \mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}^{1/2},
\]
\[
\text{Log}_{\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}}(\mathbf{C}) = \mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}^{1/2} \log(\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}^{-1/2} \mathbf{C} \mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}^{-1/2}) \mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}^{1/2}.
\]

These maps are global diffeomorphisms due to the non‑positive curvature and simple connectivity of \(\mathcal{P}(n)\).

### A.2 Tangent Space Projection and Vectorization

Given a covariance matrix \(\mathbf{C}(t)\), we compute its projection onto the tangent space at \(\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}\):

\[
\mathbf{S}(t) = \text{Log}_{\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}}(\mathbf{C}(t)).
\]

Because \(\mathbf{S}(t)\) is symmetric, we extract the upper triangular part and scale off‑diagonal elements by \(\sqrt{2}\) to preserve the Frobenius inner product, yielding a vector \(\mathbf{X}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^d\) with \(d = n(n+1)/2\). This mapping is an isometry between the tangent space (with the Frobenius inner product induced by the affine‑invariant metric) and the Euclidean space \(\mathbb{R}^d\).

### A.3 Distortion Bound

The distance between \(\mathbf{C}\) and \(\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}\) measured in the tangent space is exactly the Riemannian distance:

\[
\|\mathbf{S}\|_F = \delta_R(\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}, \mathbf{C}).
\]

Thus, after vectorization with the scaling convention, \(\|\mathbf{X}\|_2 = \|\mathbf{S}\|_F = \delta_R(\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}, \mathbf{C})\). There is no distortion; the tangent space representation is globally distance‑preserving for the affine‑invariant metric. The often‑cited third‑order error bound applies when comparing the Euclidean distance of covariance matrices (not their projections) to the Riemannian distance; but with the logarithmic map, the distance is exactly preserved. Therefore, the claim in the main text about a cubic error term is overly cautious: the projection itself introduces zero distortion. The error bound discussed in Section 3 refers to the fact that if one approximates the Riemannian distance by the Euclidean distance of the raw covariance matrices (without tangent mapping), a cubic error appears; our method uses the exact logarithmic map and thus enjoys perfect distance preservation.

**Proof of isometry:** The affine‑invariant metric at \(\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}\) is \(\langle \mathbf{S}_1, \mathbf{S}_2 \rangle_{\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}} = \operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}^{-1}\mathbf{S}_1 \mathbf{C}_{\text{ref}}^{-1}\mathbf{S}_2)\). With \(\mathbf{S}\) expressed in the basis that diagonalizes the inner product, the vectorized representation with the \(\sqrt{2}\) scaling makes the Euclidean inner product equal to this metric. Hence \(\|\mathbf{X}\|_2 = \|\mathbf{S}\|_F = \delta_R\). ∎

