## Appendix C: Byzantine‑Robust Consensus – Geometric Median and Breakdown Point

### C.1 Definition and Existence

Given a set of vectors \(\{\mathbf{u}_i\}_{i=1}^N \subset \mathbb{R}^m\), the geometric median is defined as

\[
\mathbf{m} = \arg\min_{\mathbf{u} \in \mathbb{R}^m} \sum_{i=1}^N \|\mathbf{u} - \mathbf{u}_i\|_2.
\]

The objective is strictly convex if the points are not all collinear; thus the median exists and is unique almost surely.

### C.2 Breakdown Point

The breakdown point of an estimator is the fraction of arbitrary outliers that can be tolerated before the estimate can be driven arbitrarily far. For the geometric median, the breakdown point is \(1/2\): up to \(\lfloor (N-1)/2\rfloor\) points can be corrupted without making the median unbounded, and it remains in the convex hull of the inliers.

**Proof (sketch):** Let the good set \(\mathcal{G}\) have size \(N_g > N/2\). For any candidate point \(\mathbf{m}\) far away, the gradient of the sum of distances is dominated by the inliers, pointing back toward their convex hull. Thus any minimizer must lie in the convex hull of \(\mathcal{G}\). Conversely, if \(N_b \ge N/2\), a corrupt majority can place the median arbitrarily. Hence the breakdown point is exactly \(\lceil N/2\rceil / N\), asymptotically \(1/2\). ∎

### C.3 Weiszfeld’s Algorithm and Convergence

Weiszfeld’s algorithm iteratively solves

\[
\mathbf{y}^{(k+1)} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^N \mathbf{u}_i / \|\mathbf{u}_i - \mathbf{y}^{(k)}\|_2}{\sum_{i=1}^N 1 / \|\mathbf{u}_i - \mathbf{y}^{(k)}\|_2},
\]

with the convention that if \(\mathbf{y}^{(k)}\) equals some \(\mathbf{u}_i\), we exclude that term. The algorithm converges linearly (geometric convergence rate) to the unique median, provided \(\mathbf{y}^{(0)}\) is not exactly at a data point (which can cause division by zero). In practice, a small regularization \(\epsilon\) avoids singularity. For \(m \le 10\) and \(N \le 10^4\), convergence to \(10^{-6}\) tolerance occurs in fewer than 20 iterations. Each iteration requires \(\mathcal{O}(N m)\) operations, making it suitable for real‑time fusion.

### C.4 Conflict Detection Metric

The weighted disagreement

\[
D(t) = \sum_{i,j} w_i w_j \|\hat{\mathbf{u}}_i - \hat{\mathbf{u}}_j\|^2
\]

measures the variance of the weighted distribution. It can be expressed as \(2 \text{Var}_w(\hat{\mathbf{u}})\), and thresholding it detects when the distribution is multimodal, indicating value conflicts.

