## Appendix D: Input‑to‑State Stability of Adaptive Shared Autonomy

### D.1 System Description

The closed‑loop dynamics of interest are the evolution of the AI’s internal state and the resulting error. Let the true intent \(\mathbf{u}^*(t)\) be a bounded, slowly varying signal. The AI copilot’s autonomous command \(\mathbf{u}_{\text{auto}}(t)\) is generated by a dynamical system (e.g., a model‑predictive controller) that is input‑to‑state stable (ISS) with respect to the estimation error \(\mathbf{e}_{\text{auto}} = \mathbf{u}_{\text{auto}} - \mathbf{u}^*\). Precisely, there exist \(\beta \in \mathcal{KL}\) and \(\gamma \in \mathcal{K}\) such that for all \(t \ge 0\),

\[
\|\mathbf{e}_{\text{auto}}(t)\| \le \beta(\|\mathbf{e}_{\text{auto}}(0)\|, t) + \gamma(\sup_{0\le \tau \le t}\|\mathbf{w}_{\text{auto}}(\tau)\|),
\]

where \(\mathbf{w}_{\text{auto}}\) is the AI’s own measurement/sensing noise.

### D.2 Blending Law Stability

The fused human intent is \(\bar{\mathbf{u}} = \mathbf{u}^* + \bar{\mathbf{w}}\), with \(\bar{\mathbf{w}} = \sum_i \alpha_i \mathbf{w}_i\) and \(\|\bar{\mathbf{w}}\| \le \max_i\|\mathbf{w}_i\| = B\). The final command is

\[
\mathbf{u}_{\text{final}} = \rho \bar{\mathbf{u}} + (1-\rho) \mathbf{u}_{\text{auto}} = \mathbf{u}^* + \rho \bar{\mathbf{w}} + (1-\rho) \mathbf{e}_{\text{auto}}.
\]

The error \(\mathbf{e} = \mathbf{u}_{\text{final}} - \mathbf{u}^*\) satisfies

\[
\|\mathbf{e}(t)\| \le \rho(t) B + (1-\rho(t)) \|\mathbf{e}_{\text{auto}}(t)\|.
\]

Since \(0 \le \rho(t) \le 1\), the error is a convex combination of a bounded term and an ISS term. Using the ISS property of the autonomous controller and the fact that \(\rho(t)\) is bounded and Lipschitz in the cognitive load (which is itself bounded), one can construct an ISS Lyapunov function for the cascade. A detailed proof follows from Sontag’s ISS composition theorems (Sontag, 2008). In the special case where \(\mathbf{w}_i \to 0\) and \(\mathbf{u}^*\) is constant, the AI copilot’s error \(\mathbf{e}_{\text{auto}}\) also tends to zero (by ISS with vanishing input), and thus \(\mathbf{e}(t) \to 0\). This proves that the human‑AI symbiosis is stable and recovers exact alignment when human decoders become perfect and the AI’s estimation is asymptotically exact.

